low reviewer scores (2.0-2.9), minimal claims surviving (0-1), weak novelty and soundness ratings (1.5-2.5 and 2.5-3.5 respectively), and very low acceptance probability (0.05-0.20). I'll work through each experiment systematically with these worst-tier parameters in mind. The user wants me to generate predicted experimental results for all planned experiments under the worst-tier interpretation. For experiments already completed in the registry, I'll use actual results but frame them pessimistically; for those not yet run, I'll predict worst-case outcomes. I need to work through all 13 experiments systematically, explaining for each one why the worst tier would produce those particular results, considering how reviewers would perceive limited novelty and other negative factors.\n\nNow I'm drafting the YAML output, starting with the completed experiments and moving through the planned ones, articulating how each result would be dismissed or interpreted unfavorably under worst-case reviewer assumptions. The bifactor IRT approach got abandoned because the model wouldn't converge properly. Generated items don't calibrate well to the originals, and the Bayesian simulation only shows marginal gains without addressing the underlying data issues. Item-focused decision trees reveal that DIF is actually confounded with difficulty and discrimination parameters, which undermines the core finding. The architecture stratification analysis is conceptually done but...\n\nWriting YAML output...","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":19580,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":19580},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0cc9e7fb-63a4-4d71-be25-3b8e3d4642f2","uuid":"9b3fc3b2-9c37-40be-9883-f96951bc