{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_014YEHScdre8m96eNCERjuDT","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"```yaml\ntier: worst\nnarrative_guidance: >\n  Reviewers apply a methods-paper novelty bar — \"MH-DIF is textbook psychometrics, applying it to a matrix is engineering, not research.\"\n  The 31% figure is dismissed as an artifact of ability confounding + IRT misspecification. The meta-critique is viewed as\n  an attack on the contamination detection community rather than a constructive methodological observation. The paper is seen\n  as a descriptive catalog of DIF results with no actionable prescriptive contribution. 8-page compression makes every section\n  feel superficial. One reviewer from the contamination detection community scores defensively. Consensus: \"interesting\n  exercise, but not at the level of an EMNLP long paper.\"\n\nimagined_experiments:\n  - name: real_data_dif_mmlu\n    plan_id: plan_001\n    description: \"MH-DIF on METABENCH MMLU 5219×12508, temporal/ability cohorts, Li et al. proxy validation, within-family control, domain specificity\"\n    expected_results:\n      temporal_ets_c_pct: 31.3\n      temporal_enrichment_li: 0.96\n      ability_ets_c_pct: 2.9\n      within_family_llama2v3_c_pct: 61.3\n      within_family_mistral_mixtral_c_pct: 38.7\n      domain_spearman_rho: 0.307\n      domain_spearman_p: 0.02\n      sanity_random_split_c_pct: 0.18\n      irt_misfit_pct_lz_gt2: 98.0\n    result_rationale: >\n      Numbers themselves are reproducible but reviewers in this tier attack interpretation, not arithmetic.\n      (1) enrichment≈0.96 is used as primary evidence AGAINST DIF: \"your detector is no better than random relative to\n      the only available ground truth — you've built a method that doesn't correlate with contamination.\" The nuanced\n      meta-critique (web-overlap ≠ differential contamination) is dismissed as post-hoc rationalization.\n      (2) 31.3% temporal C% vs 0.18% random sanity is acknowledged as non-random, but reviewer frames it as\n      \"31% of items get easier when models get better — that's called progress, not instability.\" The ρ=0.307 domain\n      correlation supports this reading.\n      (3) Within-family Llama-2v3 at 61.3% is treated as a fatal confound: \"your biggest within-family comparison\n      shows that capability gain alone produces massive DIF, confirming the 31% temporal finding is ability confounding.\"\n      (4) 98% IRT |Lz|>2 triggers the strongest attack: \"your entire analysis rests on a model that fits almost no\n      examinees — DIF from a misspecified model is uninterpretable. You should have started with multidimensional IRT\n      or abandoned the psychometric framing entirely.\"\n    tables:\n      - \"Table 1: DIF classification by cohort type (temporal vs ability vs random sanity) — reviewer acknowledges but\n        notes ability cohort C%=2.9% proves DIF is mainly detecting ability differences\"\n      - \"Table 2: Within-family DIF rates — reviewer cites Llama-2v3 61.3% as proof that capability improvement\n        dominates the signal\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure 1: ETS C% across cohort definitions — bar chart. Reviewer notes the temporal bar (31%) dwarfs ability (3%),\n        but argues this is because temporal cohorts conflate ability with time.\"\n    analysis: >\n      In the worst tier, plan_001 becomes the paper's biggest liability. The enrichment≈1.0 result, intended as\n      support for the meta-critique, is flipped into evidence that DIF doesn't work. The within-family Llama-2v3\n      result, intended as a confound control, becomes Exhibit A for the \"it's just ability\" argument. The 98%\n      IRT misfit is a pre-existing condition that poisons all downstream analyses. Reviewer concludes: \"The finding\n      is either trivially explained (models improve) or produced by a misspecified model (IRT doesn't fit). Either\n      way, the 31% number is not interpretable as claimed.\"\n\n  - name: rl_robustness_validation\n    plan_id: plan_002\n    description: \"Validate DIF robustness to RL-trained models: SFT baseline, alignment-RL (DPO/ORPO), reasoning-RL (GRPO/PPO)\"\n    expected_results:\n      n_reasoning_rl_models_available: 13\n      sft_baseline_c_pct: 30.8\n      alignment_rl_added_c_pct: 31.1\n      reasoning_rl_added_c_pct: 28.4\n      jaccard_sft_vs_alignment_rl: 0.72\n      jaccard_sft_vs_reasoning_rl: 0.58\n      min_k_auroc_sft: 0.61\n      min_k_auroc_reasoning_rl: 0.54\n      constat_auroc_sft: 0.58\n      constat_auroc_reasoning_rl: 0.49\n      bootstrap_ci_delong_p: 0.18\n      tost_equivalence_p: 0.09\n    result_rationale: >\n      N=13 reasoning-RL models is the core weakness. In worst tier, reviewers dismiss the entire experiment:\n      \"You tested 13 models and claim robustness to RL? That's an anecdote, not validation.\" The Jaccard drop\n      from 0.72 (alignment-RL) to 0.58 (reasoning-RL) is read as evidence of non-trivial instability, not\n      robustness. DeLong test p=0.18 is non-significant but CI is wide — reviewer notes \"failure to reject\n      is not evidence of equivalence, and your TOST at p=0.09 doesn't pass the conventional α=0.05 threshold.\"\n      Traditional detector degradation (MinK% AUROC 0.61→0.54, ConStat 0.58→0.49) is modest, undermining the\n      narrative that DIF uniquely resists RL. A contamination detection reviewer argues: \"You show marginal\n      degradation for traditional methods on 13 models. My method tested on 500+ models shows no such issue.\n      Your comparison is underpowered and misleading.\"\n    tables:\n      - \"Table: DIF flag stability across cohort compositions — reviewer dismisses as underpowered\"\n      - \"Table: Per-method AUROC with bootstrap CIs — reviewer notes overlapping CIs across all methods\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure: AUROC comparison bar chart with error bars — all CIs overlap, visual impression is 'no difference\n        between methods'\"\n    analysis: >\n      Plan_002 was already identified as underpowered (N=13) in the framing doc. In worst tier, it actively\n      hurts the paper: including it signals that the authors are aware of the RL robustness question but\n      can only muster 13 models. Better to have acknowledged the limitation than to have run a test that\n      fails to convince. The contamination detection community reviewer treats this as the weakest section\n      and uses it to question the authors' experimental rigor throughout.\n\n  - name: constat_perturbation_eval\n    plan_id: plan_003a\n    description: \"Independent ConStat-style perturbation testing on 200+ MMLU items as baseline comparison\"\n    expected_results:\n      n_items_reformulated: 220\n      n_models_tested: 45\n      constat_detection_rate_overall: 0.24\n      constat_fdr_corrected_rate: 0.18\n      constat_vs_dif_overlap_jaccard: 0.09\n      constat_precision_vs_li_labels: 0.42\n      constat_recall_vs_li_labels: 0.31\n      effect_size_original_vs_reformulated: 0.34\n    result_rationale: >\n      In worst tier, ConStat actually performs respectably on the 220 items — 24% detection pre-FDR, 18% post-FDR.\n      This creates a problem: if ConStat detects contamination at 18% and DIF detects instability at 31%, and\n      they overlap at only Jaccard=0.09, reviewer asks \"which one is right?\" The low overlap is framed not as\n      \"measuring different constructs\" (the paper's intended argument) but as \"at least one method has very high\n      false positives.\" ConStat's 0.42 precision against Li et al. labels (vs DIF's enrichment≈1.0) is used to\n      argue ConStat is the more valid detector: \"ConStat at least correlates with known contamination; DIF\n      correlates with nothing.\" The modest effect size (0.34) on reformulations suggests genuine contamination\n      signal exists and ConStat captures it. Reviewer from contamination detection community: \"This comparison\n      validates our perturbation approach and shows DIF is measuring something else entirely — probably ability\n      confounding, as we suspected.\"\n    tables:\n      - \"Table: ConStat detection rates by MMLU domain — shows plausible domain variation\"\n      - \"Table: DIF vs ConStat flag overlap matrix — Jaccard=0.09 highlighted as near-zero agreement\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure: Venn diagram of DIF-C vs ConStat flags — visually emphasizes almost no overlap\"\n    analysis: >\n      ConStat performing reasonably well is the worst-tier killer for Claim 2. The meta-critique (\"web-overlap\n      labels can't validate item-level detectors\") loses force when ConStat shows non-trivial correlation\n      with those same labels. The near-zero overlap with DIF shifts the burden of proof: reviewers ask DIF\n      to explain why it disagrees with a method that correlates with ground truth, rather than asking ConStat\n      to explain why it agrees with a flawed ground truth.\n\n  - name: dvd_generation_variance_eval\n    plan_id: plan_003b\n    description: \"DVD generation variance analysis on 10-15 open-weight models × 50 generations per item\"\n    expected_results:\n      n_models_tested: 12\n      n_items_tested: 500\n      n_generations_per_item: 50\n      bimodal_detection_rate: 0.07\n      dvd_vs_dif_overlap_jaccard: 0.05\n      dvd_vs_constat_overlap_jaccard: 0.11\n      dvd_auroc_vs_li_labels: 0.56\n      temperature_sensitivity: \"results vary >15% between T=0.7 and T=1.0\"\n      compute_cost_gpu_hours: 68\n    result_rationale: >\n      In worst tier, DVD produces weak but non-zero results (7% bimodal items), and the near-zero overlap\n      with DIF (Jaccard=0.05) reinforces the narrative that DIF is an outlier method. The 12 models tested\n      are few enough that reviewer questions generalizability, but results are just good enough to suggest\n      DVD captures a different contamination signal. Temperature sensitivity (>15% variation) is noted but\n      not fatal — reviewer says \"this shows contamination detection is sensitive to evaluation protocol,\n      which is interesting and under-explored in this paper.\" The 68 GPU-hours spent on this experiment is\n      seen as disproportionate to its contribution, and the results feel like a method survey rather than\n      a paper contribution. Reviewer: \"You've shown three methods disagree. That's not a contribution —\n      that's a problem statement.\"\n    tables:\n      - \"Table: DVD detection rates by model family — sparse, only 12 models\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure: Generation variance distributions for exemplar items — bimodal vs unimodal\"\n    analysis: >\n      DVD adds little to the paper in any tier. In worst tier, it becomes dead weight: GPU-expensive,\n      underpowered, and its results are used by reviewers to argue the field needs more careful method\n      comparison, not another method (DIF). The three-way disagreement (DIF/ConStat/DVD all flag\n      different items) is the worst possible outcome — it makes all methods look unreliable.\n\n  - name: ensemble_fusion\n    plan_id: plan_003c\n    description: \"Fuse DIF + ConStat + DVD into multi-signal ensemble with calibrated thresholds\"\n    expected_results:\n      majority_voting_precision: 0.15\n      majority_voting_recall: 0.04\n      weighted_fusion_auroc: 0.59\n      ensemble_fpr: 0.12\n      cross_validation_variance: 0.08\n      uniform_vs_optimized_delta_auroc: 0.03\n      n_items_flagged_by_all_three: 28\n    result_rationale: >\n      In worst tier, ensemble fusion fails because the constituent signals disagree fundamentally\n      (Jaccard <0.11 pairwise). Majority voting produces extremely low recall (0.04) because so few\n      items are flagged by 2+ methods. Weighted fusion AUROC of 0.59 barely exceeds chance, and\n      cross-validation variance of 0.08 means the weights are unstable. Only 28 items flagged by all\n      three methods — too few to draw conclusions. Reviewer: \"The ensemble doesn't work because the\n      methods measure different things. This is acknowledged in the paper but then the authors proceed\n      to fuse them anyway, which is methodologically incoherent. If DIF measures 'instability' and\n      ConStat measures 'contamination,' why would you ensemble them?\" The optimized weights barely\n      improve over uniform (Δ=0.03), suggesting no meaningful signal structure in the combination.\n      This experiment, dependent on 003a and 003b both working, collapses when its inputs disagree.\n    tables:\n      - \"Table: Ensemble performance under different fusion strategies — all near chance\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure: ROC curves for individual methods vs ensemble — ensemble curve barely above diagonal\"\n    analysis: >\n      Ensemble was the original Claim 2 headline. Its failure in worst tier is predictable from the\n      near-zero pairwise overlaps. The experiment serves as evidence that DIF, ConStat, and DVD are\n      incommensurable — which could be an interesting finding but is framed as a failure rather than\n      a discovery. Reviewer sees this as confirming the paper lacks a coherent detection contribution.\n\n  - name: cross_benchmark_gsm8k\n    plan_id: plan_004\n    description: \"DIF replication on GSM8K (METABENCH) + GSM1K contamination gap correlation\"\n    expected_results:\n      gsm8k_n_items: 1319\n      gsm8k_temporal_c_pct: 18.9\n      semi_synthetic_delta_tpr_p10: 0.818\n      gsm1k_n_models: 33\n      gsm1k_pearson_r: -0.478\n      gsm1k_pearson_p: 0.005\n      gsm1k_spearman_rho: -0.403\n      gsm1k_95ci_upper_abs_r: 0.155\n      gsm1k_partial_r: -0.385\n      cooks_d_max: \"Deepseek-math-7b-rl\"\n      leave_one_out_r_range: \"[-0.537, -0.374]\"\n    result_rationale: >\n      Step 1 (GSM8K DIF) and Step 2 (semi-synthetic) are solid but in worst tier reviewers focus\n      exclusively on Step 3 (GSM1K correlation). N=33 is the attack surface: \"33 models is a\n      convenience sample, not a systematic evaluation.\" The BCa CI upper bound for |r| at 0.155\n      (Pearson) and 0.031 (Spearman) means \"the true effect could be negligibly small.\" The negative\n      direction (DIF items RESIST contamination gains) is interesting but reviewers in worst tier\n      flip it: \"So DIF items are just harder items that contamination doesn't help with? That's not\n      evidence DIF detects contamination — it's evidence DIF detects difficulty.\" The item difficulty\n      confound (DIF-C items are harder, Cohen's d=-0.247 from plan_023) directly supports this\n      alternative explanation. Deepseek-math-7b-rl as the max Cook's D model raises eyebrows:\n      \"Your strongest result depends on one model specifically designed for math reasoning.\"\n      Reviewer: \"N=33, wide CIs, plausible difficulty confound, one influential outlier.\n      This is exploratory at best.\"\n    tables:\n      - \"Table: GSM1K correlation with CIs — reviewer circles the CI upper bounds\"\n      - \"Table: Leave-one-out sensitivity — shows stability but reviewer notes 'stable weak effect\n        is still weak'\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure: Scatter plot of DIF-C accuracy vs contamination gap (N=33) — reviewer notes\n        sparse cloud with possible leverage points\"\n    analysis: >\n      Plan_004 Step 3 is the only cross-benchmark validation with an independent ground truth\n      (GSM1K). In worst tier, N=33 and the difficulty confound make it suggestive rather than\n      compelling. Reviewers don't attack the direction (negative r is actually more interesting\n      than positive), but they attack the power and the alternative explanation. The semi-synthetic\n      Step 2 (ΔTPR=0.818) is strong but dismissed as \"artificial\" (same attack as plan_011).\n      Step 1 (C%=18.9% on GSM8K) is consistent with the MMLU finding but reviewer notes:\n      \"Lower rate on a single-domain benchmark is expected — this doesn't validate the MMLU finding,\n      it just shows DIF produces a number everywhere you run it.\"\n\n  - name: bifactor_irt_equating_pilot\n    plan_id: plan_005\n    description: \"Bifactor IRT model on MMLU (general + subject factors), anchor item selection, fixed-parameter calibration\"\n    expected_results:\n      bifactor_convergence: false\n      rmsea: \">0.10 (poor fit)\"\n      cfi: \"<0.80 (poor fit)\"\n      n_items_attempted: 12508\n      n_subjects_as_factors: 57\n      estimation_time_hours: 48\n      anchor_items_identified: 0\n      equating_completed: false\n    result_rationale: >\n      In worst tier, the bifactor model fails to converge on 57 subject-specific factors + 1 general\n      factor for 12,508 items. This is computationally expected — bifactor IRT with 58 latent dimensions\n      and 12K items requires enormous sample sizes and careful specification. Even if forced to converge,\n      RMSEA>0.10 and CFI<0.80 indicate poor fit, meaning the factor structure doesn't capture MMLU's\n      complexity. No anchor items can be identified because the model doesn't fit, so equating never\n      starts. This is consistent with the plan_015 outcome (killed at PREPARING stage). Reviewer:\n      \"The authors attempted IRT equating but their model doesn't fit — this confirms IRT is\n      fundamentally misspecified for MMLU, which undermines their entire DIF analysis (which also\n      rests on IRT assumptions). They acknowledge this but proceed anyway.\" The failure is\n      self-damaging: it provides direct evidence against the paper's own methodology.\n    tables: []\n    figures_description: []\n    analysis: >\n      Plan_005 is a known failure (killed as plan_015). In worst tier, reviewers cite it as evidence\n      of methodological over-reach: the authors know IRT doesn't fit MMLU (98% |Lz|>2, bifactor\n      fails) but continue to use IRT-derived MH-DIF. This is the most damaging self-inflicted wound\n      in the paper. The planned equating (Claim 3 original) is dead. Even in the current framing\n      where equating is dropped, a reviewer who notices the bifactor attempt will ask: \"If bifactor\n      IRT can't model MMLU, why should we trust MH-DIF, which conditions on a unidimensional\n      total score?\"\n\n  - name: item_generation_calibration\n    plan_id: plan_006\n    description: \"LLM-generated replacement items for DIF-flagged questions, calibrated via student simulation\"\n    expected_results:\n      n_items_generated: 300\n      difficulty_correlation_prompt_based: 0.18\n      difficulty_correlation_student_sim: 0.52\n      target_difficulty_mae: 0.21\n      construct_coverage_maintained: false\n      holdout_model_panel_rho: 0.72\n      expert_quality_rating: \"2.8/5 (below acceptable threshold)\"\n    result_rationale: >\n      In worst tier, item generation fails at the calibration stage. Prompt-based difficulty targeting\n      (ρ=0.18) is near the lower bound of published estimates (0.10-0.45). Student simulation improves\n      to ρ=0.52 but remains well below the r=0.75-0.82 reported in the source paper (arXiv 2601.09953),\n      likely because MMLU's 57-domain breadth is harder to simulate than narrow-domain tests. Generated\n      items don't maintain construct coverage: reviewers note that LLM-generated questions cluster in\n      easily-generatable knowledge areas, leaving specialized domains under-represented. The holdout\n      validation (ρ=0.72) is below the 0.95 threshold specified in the plan. Reviewer: \"The authors\n      can't even generate replacement items that correlate above 0.75 with the originals. This\n      undermines the practical value of detecting unstable items — if you can't replace them,\n      knowing they're unstable is academic.\"\n    tables:\n      - \"Table: Difficulty correlation by calibration method — both below targets\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure: Original vs generated item difficulty scatter — loose cloud, far from diagonal\"\n    analysis: >\n      Plan_006 depends on plan_005 (bifactor equating) which already failed. Without a working\n      equating framework, replacement items cannot be properly calibrated. In worst tier, this\n      experiment embodies the reviewer critique \"interesting observation, but what do we do with it?\"\n      — the answer is \"we tried to do something, and it didn't work well enough.\" This reinforces\n      the purely-descriptive characterization of the paper.\n\n  - name: cumulative_bayesian_dif\n    plan_id: plan_007\n    description: \"Bayesian DIF priors (Sinharay et al. 2006) across 4 simulated benchmark administrations\"\n    expected_results:\n      single_admin_power_d10: 0.38\n      cumulative_2_admin_power_d10: 0.49\n      cumulative_3_admin_power_d10: 0.58\n      cumulative_4_admin_power_d10: 0.64\n      target_power_0_70_achieved: false\n      large_effect_d14_cumulative_4: 0.73\n      simulation_only: true\n      real_data_validation: none\n    result_rationale: >\n      In worst tier, the Bayesian cumulative approach shows improvement over single administrations\n      but falls short of the target 0.70 power for medium effects (δ=1.0) after 4 administrations —\n      reaching only 0.64. This is because the MMLU item parameters used in the simulation reflect\n      the misspecified 1D IRT model, propagating bias into the Bayesian priors. For large effects\n      (δ=1.4), cumulative power of 0.73 barely clears the threshold, but reviewers note: \"You already\n      showed in phase 2 that single-admin MH achieves 0.68 at δ=1.4 — the Bayesian approach adds\n      massive complexity for 5 percentage points.\" The simulation-only nature (no real multi-wave\n      benchmark data exists) is fatal: \"This is a simulation study within a simulation — you\n      simulated items from a misspecified model, then simulated multiple administrations that\n      don't exist. There are no real data to validate any of this.\"\n    tables:\n      - \"Table: Power by cumulative administrations and effect size — incremental gains flatten\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure: Power curves across administrations — diminishing returns visible\"\n    analysis: >\n      Plan_007 is pure simulation with no empirical anchor. In worst tier, it is the most\n      expendable section — reviewers see it as speculative future work masquerading as a result.\n      The 8-page constraint means including this pushes out content that could address real\n      reviewer concerns. Framing doc already marked this as appendix material; in worst tier,\n      even appendix inclusion is questioned as padding.\n\n  - name: graceful_degradation_analysis\n    plan_id: plan_008\n    description: \"Benchmark validity degradation under progressive item replacement (5-50%)\"\n    expected_results:\n      rho_5pct_replacement: 0.998\n      rho_10pct_replacement: 0.995\n      rho_20pct_replacement: 0.988\n      rho_50pct_replacement: 0.962\n      reliability_drop_at_31pct: 0.006\n      construct_coverage_loss_at_31pct: \"4 of 57 subjects lose >50% items\"\n      validity_lower_bound_analytical: 0.94\n    result_rationale: >\n      In worst tier, graceful degradation actually works TOO well, undermining the paper's\n      significance. ρ=0.988 at 20% replacement and 0.962 at 50% shows MMLU is massively redundant.\n      Reviewers use this against Claim 1: \"You've shown that removing 31% of items barely moves\n      rankings (ρ≈0.997 from plan_017). You've now shown that replacing up to 50% of items only\n      drops ρ to 0.962. So the 31% instability doesn't matter — the benchmark is robust to it.\n      Why should anyone care?\" The 4 subjects losing >50% items at 31% replacement is a minor\n      concern but reviewer notes these are small subjects (N<50 items) already statistically\n      unreliable. This experiment provides ammunition for the \"instability ≠ invalidity\"\n      framing but reviewers in worst tier turn it into \"instability AND invalidity are both\n      non-issues, so what's the contribution?\"\n    tables:\n      - \"Table: Ranking stability metrics by replacement rate — all high\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure: ρ degradation curve — nearly flat until 50%, then gentle decline\"\n    analysis: >\n      Graceful degradation is a double-edged sword. In best tiers, it supports the\n      \"instability ≠ invalidity\" framing as nuanced. In worst tier, it's used to argue the\n      entire paper investigates a non-problem. Reviewer: \"MMLU tolerates 50% item replacement\n      with ρ>0.96. Your 31% instability finding is interesting but inconsequential. The benchmark\n      works fine. Move on.\"\n\n  - name: item_focused_trees\n    plan_id: plan_009\n    description: \"Item-Focused Trees (Tutz & Berger 2016) — DIF with continuous release-date covariate\"\n    expected_results:\n      n_items_with_significant_splits: 3421\n      pct_items_with_splits: 27.4\n      concordance_with_mh_dif_c: 0.61\n      primary_split_variable: \"release_date\"\n      secondary_splits: \"model_size (19%), training_data_cutoff (12%)\"\n      tree_depth_median: 2\n      overfitting_cv_auc_drop: 0.14\n    result_rationale: >\n      In worst tier, Item-Focused Trees reveal that model release date is the primary split for\n      27.4% of items — consistent with temporal DIF but concordance with MH (0.61) is moderate,\n      not high. This opens an attack: \"If a more flexible method agrees with MH on only 61%\n      of flags, MH is either over- or under-detecting.\" Secondary splits on model_size (19%)\n      and training_data_cutoff (12%) suggest DIF is partially confounded with these covariates,\n      not purely temporal. The cv_auc drop of 0.14 indicates overfitting, which reviewers use\n      to question tree-based results. Most damagingly, reviewer notes: \"If release date is the\n      primary DIF driver and model size is secondary, you're detecting capability scaling, not\n      contamination or instability. These trees just rediscover that bigger, newer models do\n      better on certain items.\" The continuous-covariate approach, intended to avoid arbitrary\n      cohort splits, instead reveals that the underlying signal is smooth capability growth,\n      not discrete item-level dysfunction.\n    tables:\n      - \"Table: Split variable importance across items — release_date dominates\"\n      - \"Table: MH vs IFT flag concordance matrix\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure: Exemplar item trees showing release_date and model_size splits — reviewer\n        reads as 'scaling laws, not contamination'\"\n    analysis: >\n      Item-Focused Trees are a robustness check that in worst tier becomes a liability. The\n      moderate MH concordance and the model_size secondary splits give reviewers a clean\n      alternative narrative: \"DIF detects where models improved, which correlates with when\n      they were released and how big they are. This is capability scaling, observable from\n      accuracy trends alone, and requires no psychometric machinery.\" The IFT results\n      paradoxically make the simpler explanation (capability growth) more plausible than the\n      DIF interpretation (item-level functional instability).\n\n  - name: architecture_stratified_dif\n    plan_id: plan_010\n    description: \"DIF within decoder-only subpopulation to test IRT fit improvement when architecture is homogeneous\"\n    expected_results:\n      n_decoder_only: 5226\n      n_non_decoder: 1\n      pct_decoder_only: 99.98\n      c_pct_decoder_only: 31.3\n      c_pct_full: 31.3\n      jaccard_decoder_only_vs_full: 1.0\n      irt_rmsea_decoder_only: 0.089\n      irt_rmsea_full: 0.091\n      irt_misfit_pct_decoder_only: 97.8\n      fit_improvement: \"negligible\"\n    result_rationale: >\n      In worst tier, this experiment backfires. The finding that METABENCH MMLU is 99.98%\n      decoder-only (5226/5227) means the architecture stratification is trivial — removing\n      one model changes nothing (Jaccard=1.0). Reviewer: \"This isn't an experiment, it's a\n      data description. You showed your dataset has one non-decoder model. The fact that you\n      needed a whole experiment to discover this suggests insufficient dataset exploration\n      before running analyses.\" More importantly, IRT misfit barely improves (97.8% vs 98.0%)\n      in the decoder-only subset, confirming that architecture heterogeneity was never the\n      source of misfit — multidimensionality is. This should have been the paper's primary\n      concern from the start. Reviewer: \"Architecture homogeneity doesn't help because the\n      problem is 57 subject domains, not architecture. You've ruled out the wrong confounder.\"\n    tables:\n      - \"Table: Architecture composition of METABENCH — one row, one number\"\n    figures_description: []\n    analysis: >\n      Plan_010 was designed as a proactive defense against the IRT misfit concern. In worst\n      tier, it proves the defense is irrelevant: the IRT misfit isn't from architecture\n      heterogeneity (which doesn't exist) but from MMLU's inherent multidimensionality\n      (which can't be fixed by subsetting). The experiment answers the wrong question and\n      wastes precious page space on a tautological result. Reviewer correctly identifies\n      that the real issue — multidimensional IRT — is acknowledged but never attempted.\n\n  - name: semi_synthetic_contamination_validation\n    plan_id: plan_011\n    description: \"Inject known contamination into METABENCH response matrix, measure DIF detection power\"\n    expected_results:\n      n_clean_items_selected: 500\n      n_items_injected: 200\n      temporal_external_delta_tpr_p05: 0.508\n      temporal_external_delta_tpr_p10: 0.674\n      temporal_external_fpr: 0.314\n      dose_response_p03_tpr: 0.548\n      dose_response_p07_tpr: 0.990\n      dose_response_p10_tpr: 1.000\n      fpr_inflation_standard_mh: \"0.298→0.616 at p=1.0\"\n      matching_variable_contamination: true\n    result_rationale: >\n      In worst tier, the semi-synthetic results are numerically strong (ΔTPR=+0.508, clear dose-response)\n      but reviewers attack the injection mechanism itself. \"Flipping incorrect→correct with probability p\n      is a caricature of contamination. Real contamination manifests through training data exposure, which\n      can produce partial memorization, template matching, or reasoning shortcuts — none of which look\n      like a coin flip.\" The baseline FPR of 0.314 (31.4% of clean items flagged before injection) is\n      treated as disqualifying: \"Your method has a 31% false positive rate on items you selected as clean.\n      Any TPR increase is measured against an already-noisy baseline.\" The FPR inflation of standard MH\n      (0.298→0.616) when injection contaminates the matching variable is noted but reviewer argues:\n      \"This means your method is fragile to exactly the kind of correlated contamination that occurs in\n      practice.\" The contamination detection community reviewer: \"Semi-synthetic injection is the weakest\n      form of validation — we stopped using it years ago in favor of real contamination benchmarks.\n      This is validation theater.\"\n    tables:\n      - \"Table: ΔTPR dose-response curve — reviewer acknowledges monotonicity but questions mechanism\"\n      - \"Table: FPR inflation under standard vs external matching — reviewer focuses on baseline FPR\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure: Dose-response curve (injection rate vs TPR) — clean monotonic increase, but\n        reviewer annotates the 31% baseline FPR line\"\n    analysis: >\n      Plan_011 is the paper's strongest controlled experiment, but in worst tier it's\n      undermined by three attacks: (1) injection mechanism unrealistic, (2) baseline FPR=31%\n      meaning the method flags ~1/3 of clean items, (3) matching variable contamination\n      shows fragility. The 31% baseline FPR is the same 31% temporal C% from plan_001 —\n      reviewer connects these: \"Your 'semi-synthetic clean items' still show 31% DIF because\n      your method has a 31% base rate. Injection just adds more flags on top. The dose-response\n      proves injection works as a perturbation, not that DIF detects contamination.\" This is\n      the most sophisticated attack in the worst tier — it reframes the entire semi-synthetic\n      framework as measuring perturbation sensitivity, not contamination detection.\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":19580,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":19580},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0cc9e7fb-63a4-4d71-be25-3b8e3d4642f2","uuid":"98ad394e-c03a-43e6-a784-c471f7edca4b"}
{"type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"api_error_status":null,"duration_ms":249882,"duration_api_ms":251472,"num_turns":1,"result":"```yaml\ntier: worst\nnarrative_guidance: >\n  Reviewers apply a methods-paper novelty bar — \"MH-DIF is textbook psychometrics, applying it to a matrix is engineering, not research.\"\n  The 31% figure is dismissed as an artifact of ability confounding + IRT misspecification. The meta-critique is viewed as\n  an attack on the contamination detection community rather than a constructive methodological observation. The paper is seen\n  as a descriptive catalog of DIF results with no actionable prescriptive contribution. 8-page compression makes every section\n  feel superficial. One reviewer from the contamination detection community scores defensively. Consensus: \"interesting\n  exercise, but not at the level of an EMNLP long paper.\"\n\nimagined_experiments:\n  - name: real_data_dif_mmlu\n    plan_id: plan_001\n    description: \"MH-DIF on METABENCH MMLU 5219×12508, temporal/ability cohorts, Li et al. proxy validation, within-family control, domain specificity\"\n    expected_results:\n      temporal_ets_c_pct: 31.3\n      temporal_enrichment_li: 0.96\n      ability_ets_c_pct: 2.9\n      within_family_llama2v3_c_pct: 61.3\n      within_family_mistral_mixtral_c_pct: 38.7\n      domain_spearman_rho: 0.307\n      domain_spearman_p: 0.02\n      sanity_random_split_c_pct: 0.18\n      irt_misfit_pct_lz_gt2: 98.0\n    result_rationale: >\n      Numbers themselves are reproducible but reviewers in this tier attack interpretation, not arithmetic.\n      (1) enrichment≈0.96 is used as primary evidence AGAINST DIF: \"your detector is no better than random relative to\n      the only available ground truth — you've built a method that doesn't correlate with contamination.\" The nuanced\n      meta-critique (web-overlap ≠ differential contamination) is dismissed as post-hoc rationalization.\n      (2) 31.3% temporal C% vs 0.18% random sanity is acknowledged as non-random, but reviewer frames it as\n      \"31% of items get easier when models get better — that's called progress, not instability.\" The ρ=0.307 domain\n      correlation supports this reading.\n      (3) Within-family Llama-2v3 at 61.3% is treated as a fatal confound: \"your biggest within-family comparison\n      shows that capability gain alone produces massive DIF, confirming the 31% temporal finding is ability confounding.\"\n      (4) 98% IRT |Lz|>2 triggers the strongest attack: \"your entire analysis rests on a model that fits almost no\n      examinees — DIF from a misspecified model is uninterpretable. You should have started with multidimensional IRT\n      or abandoned the psychometric framing entirely.\"\n    tables:\n      - \"Table 1: DIF classification by cohort type (temporal vs ability vs random sanity) — reviewer acknowledges but\n        notes ability cohort C%=2.9% proves DIF is mainly detecting ability differences\"\n      - \"Table 2: Within-family DIF rates — reviewer cites Llama-2v3 61.3% as proof that capability improvement\n        dominates the signal\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure 1: ETS C% across cohort definitions — bar chart. Reviewer notes the temporal bar (31%) dwarfs ability (3%),\n        but argues this is because temporal cohorts conflate ability with time.\"\n    analysis: >\n      In the worst tier, plan_001 becomes the paper's biggest liability. The enrichment≈1.0 result, intended as\n      support for the meta-critique, is flipped into evidence that DIF doesn't work. The within-family Llama-2v3\n      result, intended as a confound control, becomes Exhibit A for the \"it's just ability\" argument. The 98%\n      IRT misfit is a pre-existing condition that poisons all downstream analyses. Reviewer concludes: \"The finding\n      is either trivially explained (models improve) or produced by a misspecified model (IRT doesn't fit). Either\n      way, the 31% number is not interpretable as claimed.\"\n\n  - name: rl_robustness_validation\n    plan_id: plan_002\n    description: \"Validate DIF robustness to RL-trained models: SFT baseline, alignment-RL (DPO/ORPO), reasoning-RL (GRPO/PPO)\"\n    expected_results:\n      n_reasoning_rl_models_available: 13\n      sft_baseline_c_pct: 30.8\n      alignment_rl_added_c_pct: 31.1\n      reasoning_rl_added_c_pct: 28.4\n      jaccard_sft_vs_alignment_rl: 0.72\n      jaccard_sft_vs_reasoning_rl: 0.58\n      min_k_auroc_sft: 0.61\n      min_k_auroc_reasoning_rl: 0.54\n      constat_auroc_sft: 0.58\n      constat_auroc_reasoning_rl: 0.49\n      bootstrap_ci_delong_p: 0.18\n      tost_equivalence_p: 0.09\n    result_rationale: >\n      N=13 reasoning-RL models is the core weakness. In worst tier, reviewers dismiss the entire experiment:\n      \"You tested 13 models and claim robustness to RL? That's an anecdote, not validation.\" The Jaccard drop\n      from 0.72 (alignment-RL) to 0.58 (reasoning-RL) is read as evidence of non-trivial instability, not\n      robustness. DeLong test p=0.18 is non-significant but CI is wide — reviewer notes \"failure to reject\n      is not evidence of equivalence, and your TOST at p=0.09 doesn't pass the conventional α=0.05 threshold.\"\n      Traditional detector degradation (MinK% AUROC 0.61→0.54, ConStat 0.58→0.49) is modest, undermining the\n      narrative that DIF uniquely resists RL. A contamination detection reviewer argues: \"You show marginal\n      degradation for traditional methods on 13 models. My method tested on 500+ models shows no such issue.\n      Your comparison is underpowered and misleading.\"\n    tables:\n      - \"Table: DIF flag stability across cohort compositions — reviewer dismisses as underpowered\"\n      - \"Table: Per-method AUROC with bootstrap CIs — reviewer notes overlapping CIs across all methods\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure: AUROC comparison bar chart with error bars — all CIs overlap, visual impression is 'no difference\n        between methods'\"\n    analysis: >\n      Plan_002 was already identified as underpowered (N=13) in the framing doc. In worst tier, it actively\n      hurts the paper: including it signals that the authors are aware of the RL robustness question but\n      can only muster 13 models. Better to have acknowledged the limitation than to have run a test that\n      fails to convince. The contamination detection community reviewer treats this as the weakest section\n      and uses it to question the authors' experimental rigor throughout.\n\n  - name: constat_perturbation_eval\n    plan_id: plan_003a\n    description: \"Independent ConStat-style perturbation testing on 200+ MMLU items as baseline comparison\"\n    expected_results:\n      n_items_reformulated: 220\n      n_models_tested: 45\n      constat_detection_rate_overall: 0.24\n      constat_fdr_corrected_rate: 0.18\n      constat_vs_dif_overlap_jaccard: 0.09\n      constat_precision_vs_li_labels: 0.42\n      constat_recall_vs_li_labels: 0.31\n      effect_size_original_vs_reformulated: 0.34\n    result_rationale: >\n      In worst tier, ConStat actually performs respectably on the 220 items — 24% detection pre-FDR, 18% post-FDR.\n      This creates a problem: if ConStat detects contamination at 18% and DIF detects instability at 31%, and\n      they overlap at only Jaccard=0.09, reviewer asks \"which one is right?\" The low overlap is framed not as\n      \"measuring different constructs\" (the paper's intended argument) but as \"at least one method has very high\n      false positives.\" ConStat's 0.42 precision against Li et al. labels (vs DIF's enrichment≈1.0) is used to\n      argue ConStat is the more valid detector: \"ConStat at least correlates with known contamination; DIF\n      correlates with nothing.\" The modest effect size (0.34) on reformulations suggests genuine contamination\n      signal exists and ConStat captures it. Reviewer from contamination detection community: \"This comparison\n      validates our perturbation approach and shows DIF is measuring something else entirely — probably ability\n      confounding, as we suspected.\"\n    tables:\n      - \"Table: ConStat detection rates by MMLU domain — shows plausible domain variation\"\n      - \"Table: DIF vs ConStat flag overlap matrix — Jaccard=0.09 highlighted as near-zero agreement\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure: Venn diagram of DIF-C vs ConStat flags — visually emphasizes almost no overlap\"\n    analysis: >\n      ConStat performing reasonably well is the worst-tier killer for Claim 2. The meta-critique (\"web-overlap\n      labels can't validate item-level detectors\") loses force when ConStat shows non-trivial correlation\n      with those same labels. The near-zero overlap with DIF shifts the burden of proof: reviewers ask DIF\n      to explain why it disagrees with a method that correlates with ground truth, rather than asking ConStat\n      to explain why it agrees with a flawed ground truth.\n\n  - name: dvd_generation_variance_eval\n    plan_id: plan_003b\n    description: \"DVD generation variance analysis on 10-15 open-weight models × 50 generations per item\"\n    expected_results:\n      n_models_tested: 12\n      n_items_tested: 500\n      n_generations_per_item: 50\n      bimodal_detection_rate: 0.07\n      dvd_vs_dif_overlap_jaccard: 0.05\n      dvd_vs_constat_overlap_jaccard: 0.11\n      dvd_auroc_vs_li_labels: 0.56\n      temperature_sensitivity: \"results vary >15% between T=0.7 and T=1.0\"\n      compute_cost_gpu_hours: 68\n    result_rationale: >\n      In worst tier, DVD produces weak but non-zero results (7% bimodal items), and the near-zero overlap\n      with DIF (Jaccard=0.05) reinforces the narrative that DIF is an outlier method. The 12 models tested\n      are few enough that reviewer questions generalizability, but results are just good enough to suggest\n      DVD captures a different contamination signal. Temperature sensitivity (>15% variation) is noted but\n      not fatal — reviewer says \"this shows contamination detection is sensitive to evaluation protocol,\n      which is interesting and under-explored in this paper.\" The 68 GPU-hours spent on this experiment is\n      seen as disproportionate to its contribution, and the results feel like a method survey rather than\n      a paper contribution. Reviewer: \"You've shown three methods disagree. That's not a contribution —\n      that's a problem statement.\"\n    tables:\n      - \"Table: DVD detection rates by model family — sparse, only 12 models\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure: Generation variance distributions for exemplar items — bimodal vs unimodal\"\n    analysis: >\n      DVD adds little to the paper in any tier. In worst tier, it becomes dead weight: GPU-expensive,\n      underpowered, and its results are used by reviewers to argue the field needs more careful method\n      comparison, not another method (DIF). The three-way disagreement (DIF/ConStat/DVD all flag\n      different items) is the worst possible outcome — it makes all methods look unreliable.\n\n  - name: ensemble_fusion\n    plan_id: plan_003c\n    description: \"Fuse DIF + ConStat + DVD into multi-signal ensemble with calibrated thresholds\"\n    expected_results:\n      majority_voting_precision: 0.15\n      majority_voting_recall: 0.04\n      weighted_fusion_auroc: 0.59\n      ensemble_fpr: 0.12\n      cross_validation_variance: 0.08\n      uniform_vs_optimized_delta_auroc: 0.03\n      n_items_flagged_by_all_three: 28\n    result_rationale: >\n      In worst tier, ensemble fusion fails because the constituent signals disagree fundamentally\n      (Jaccard <0.11 pairwise). Majority voting produces extremely low recall (0.04) because so few\n      items are flagged by 2+ methods. Weighted fusion AUROC of 0.59 barely exceeds chance, and\n      cross-validation variance of 0.08 means the weights are unstable. Only 28 items flagged by all\n      three methods — too few to draw conclusions. Reviewer: \"The ensemble doesn't work because the\n      methods measure different things. This is acknowledged in the paper but then the authors proceed\n      to fuse them anyway, which is methodologically incoherent. If DIF measures 'instability' and\n      ConStat measures 'contamination,' why would you ensemble them?\" The optimized weights barely\n      improve over uniform (Δ=0.03), suggesting no meaningful signal structure in the combination.\n      This experiment, dependent on 003a and 003b both working, collapses when its inputs disagree.\n    tables:\n      - \"Table: Ensemble performance under different fusion strategies — all near chance\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure: ROC curves for individual methods vs ensemble — ensemble curve barely above diagonal\"\n    analysis: >\n      Ensemble was the original Claim 2 headline. Its failure in worst tier is predictable from the\n      near-zero pairwise overlaps. The experiment serves as evidence that DIF, ConStat, and DVD are\n      incommensurable — which could be an interesting finding but is framed as a failure rather than\n      a discovery. Reviewer sees this as confirming the paper lacks a coherent detection contribution.\n\n  - name: cross_benchmark_gsm8k\n    plan_id: plan_004\n    description: \"DIF replication on GSM8K (METABENCH) + GSM1K contamination gap correlation\"\n    expected_results:\n      gsm8k_n_items: 1319\n      gsm8k_temporal_c_pct: 18.9\n      semi_synthetic_delta_tpr_p10: 0.818\n      gsm1k_n_models: 33\n      gsm1k_pearson_r: -0.478\n      gsm1k_pearson_p: 0.005\n      gsm1k_spearman_rho: -0.403\n      gsm1k_95ci_upper_abs_r: 0.155\n      gsm1k_partial_r: -0.385\n      cooks_d_max: \"Deepseek-math-7b-rl\"\n      leave_one_out_r_range: \"[-0.537, -0.374]\"\n    result_rationale: >\n      Step 1 (GSM8K DIF) and Step 2 (semi-synthetic) are solid but in worst tier reviewers focus\n      exclusively on Step 3 (GSM1K correlation). N=33 is the attack surface: \"33 models is a\n      convenience sample, not a systematic evaluation.\" The BCa CI upper bound for |r| at 0.155\n      (Pearson) and 0.031 (Spearman) means \"the true effect could be negligibly small.\" The negative\n      direction (DIF items RESIST contamination gains) is interesting but reviewers in worst tier\n      flip it: \"So DIF items are just harder items that contamination doesn't help with? That's not\n      evidence DIF detects contamination — it's evidence DIF detects difficulty.\" The item difficulty\n      confound (DIF-C items are harder, Cohen's d=-0.247 from plan_023) directly supports this\n      alternative explanation. Deepseek-math-7b-rl as the max Cook's D model raises eyebrows:\n      \"Your strongest result depends on one model specifically designed for math reasoning.\"\n      Reviewer: \"N=33, wide CIs, plausible difficulty confound, one influential outlier.\n      This is exploratory at best.\"\n    tables:\n      - \"Table: GSM1K correlation with CIs — reviewer circles the CI upper bounds\"\n      - \"Table: Leave-one-out sensitivity — shows stability but reviewer notes 'stable weak effect\n        is still weak'\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure: Scatter plot of DIF-C accuracy vs contamination gap (N=33) — reviewer notes\n        sparse cloud with possible leverage points\"\n    analysis: >\n      Plan_004 Step 3 is the only cross-benchmark validation with an independent ground truth\n      (GSM1K). In worst tier, N=33 and the difficulty confound make it suggestive rather than\n      compelling. Reviewers don't attack the direction (negative r is actually more interesting\n      than positive), but they attack the power and the alternative explanation. The semi-synthetic\n      Step 2 (ΔTPR=0.818) is strong but dismissed as \"artificial\" (same attack as plan_011).\n      Step 1 (C%=18.9% on GSM8K) is consistent with the MMLU finding but reviewer notes:\n      \"Lower rate on a single-domain benchmark is expected — this doesn't validate the MMLU finding,\n      it just shows DIF produces a number everywhere you run it.\"\n\n  - name: bifactor_irt_equating_pilot\n    plan_id: plan_005\n    description: \"Bifactor IRT model on MMLU (general + subject factors), anchor item selection, fixed-parameter calibration\"\n    expected_results:\n      bifactor_convergence: false\n      rmsea: \">0.10 (poor fit)\"\n      cfi: \"<0.80 (poor fit)\"\n      n_items_attempted: 12508\n      n_subjects_as_factors: 57\n      estimation_time_hours: 48\n      anchor_items_identified: 0\n      equating_completed: false\n    result_rationale: >\n      In worst tier, the bifactor model fails to converge on 57 subject-specific factors + 1 general\n      factor for 12,508 items. This is computationally expected — bifactor IRT with 58 latent dimensions\n      and 12K items requires enormous sample sizes and careful specification. Even if forced to converge,\n      RMSEA>0.10 and CFI<0.80 indicate poor fit, meaning the factor structure doesn't capture MMLU's\n      complexity. No anchor items can be identified because the model doesn't fit, so equating never\n      starts. This is consistent with the plan_015 outcome (killed at PREPARING stage). Reviewer:\n      \"The authors attempted IRT equating but their model doesn't fit — this confirms IRT is\n      fundamentally misspecified for MMLU, which undermines their entire DIF analysis (which also\n      rests on IRT assumptions). They acknowledge this but proceed anyway.\" The failure is\n      self-damaging: it provides direct evidence against the paper's own methodology.\n    tables: []\n    figures_description: []\n    analysis: >\n      Plan_005 is a known failure (killed as plan_015). In worst tier, reviewers cite it as evidence\n      of methodological over-reach: the authors know IRT doesn't fit MMLU (98% |Lz|>2, bifactor\n      fails) but continue to use IRT-derived MH-DIF. This is the most damaging self-inflicted wound\n      in the paper. The planned equating (Claim 3 original) is dead. Even in the current framing\n      where equating is dropped, a reviewer who notices the bifactor attempt will ask: \"If bifactor\n      IRT can't model MMLU, why should we trust MH-DIF, which conditions on a unidimensional\n      total score?\"\n\n  - name: item_generation_calibration\n    plan_id: plan_006\n    description: \"LLM-generated replacement items for DIF-flagged questions, calibrated via student simulation\"\n    expected_results:\n      n_items_generated: 300\n      difficulty_correlation_prompt_based: 0.18\n      difficulty_correlation_student_sim: 0.52\n      target_difficulty_mae: 0.21\n      construct_coverage_maintained: false\n      holdout_model_panel_rho: 0.72\n      expert_quality_rating: \"2.8/5 (below acceptable threshold)\"\n    result_rationale: >\n      In worst tier, item generation fails at the calibration stage. Prompt-based difficulty targeting\n      (ρ=0.18) is near the lower bound of published estimates (0.10-0.45). Student simulation improves\n      to ρ=0.52 but remains well below the r=0.75-0.82 reported in the source paper (arXiv 2601.09953),\n      likely because MMLU's 57-domain breadth is harder to simulate than narrow-domain tests. Generated\n      items don't maintain construct coverage: reviewers note that LLM-generated questions cluster in\n      easily-generatable knowledge areas, leaving specialized domains under-represented. The holdout\n      validation (ρ=0.72) is below the 0.95 threshold specified in the plan. Reviewer: \"The authors\n      can't even generate replacement items that correlate above 0.75 with the originals. This\n      undermines the practical value of detecting unstable items — if you can't replace them,\n      knowing they're unstable is academic.\"\n    tables:\n      - \"Table: Difficulty correlation by calibration method — both below targets\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure: Original vs generated item difficulty scatter — loose cloud, far from diagonal\"\n    analysis: >\n      Plan_006 depends on plan_005 (bifactor equating) which already failed. Without a working\n      equating framework, replacement items cannot be properly calibrated. In worst tier, this\n      experiment embodies the reviewer critique \"interesting observation, but what do we do with it?\"\n      — the answer is \"we tried to do something, and it didn't work well enough.\" This reinforces\n      the purely-descriptive characterization of the paper.\n\n  - name: cumulative_bayesian_dif\n    plan_id: plan_007\n    description: \"Bayesian DIF priors (Sinharay et al. 2006) across 4 simulated benchmark administrations\"\n    expected_results:\n      single_admin_power_d10: 0.38\n      cumulative_2_admin_power_d10: 0.49\n      cumulative_3_admin_power_d10: 0.58\n      cumulative_4_admin_power_d10: 0.64\n      target_power_0_70_achieved: false\n      large_effect_d14_cumulative_4: 0.73\n      simulation_only: true\n      real_data_validation: none\n    result_rationale: >\n      In worst tier, the Bayesian cumulative approach shows improvement over single administrations\n      but falls short of the target 0.70 power for medium effects (δ=1.0) after 4 administrations —\n      reaching only 0.64. This is because the MMLU item parameters used in the simulation reflect\n      the misspecified 1D IRT model, propagating bias into the Bayesian priors. For large effects\n      (δ=1.4), cumulative power of 0.73 barely clears the threshold, but reviewers note: \"You already\n      showed in phase 2 that single-admin MH achieves 0.68 at δ=1.4 — the Bayesian approach adds\n      massive complexity for 5 percentage points.\" The simulation-only nature (no real multi-wave\n      benchmark data exists) is fatal: \"This is a simulation study within a simulation — you\n      simulated items from a misspecified model, then simulated multiple administrations that\n      don't exist. There are no real data to validate any of this.\"\n    tables:\n      - \"Table: Power by cumulative administrations and effect size — incremental gains flatten\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure: Power curves across administrations — diminishing returns visible\"\n    analysis: >\n      Plan_007 is pure simulation with no empirical anchor. In worst tier, it is the most\n      expendable section — reviewers see it as speculative future work masquerading as a result.\n      The 8-page constraint means including this pushes out content that could address real\n      reviewer concerns. Framing doc already marked this as appendix material; in worst tier,\n      even appendix inclusion is questioned as padding.\n\n  - name: graceful_degradation_analysis\n    plan_id: plan_008\n    description: \"Benchmark validity degradation under progressive item replacement (5-50%)\"\n    expected_results:\n      rho_5pct_replacement: 0.998\n      rho_10pct_replacement: 0.995\n      rho_20pct_replacement: 0.988\n      rho_50pct_replacement: 0.962\n      reliability_drop_at_31pct: 0.006\n      construct_coverage_loss_at_31pct: \"4 of 57 subjects lose >50% items\"\n      validity_lower_bound_analytical: 0.94\n    result_rationale: >\n      In worst tier, graceful degradation actually works TOO well, undermining the paper's\n      significance. ρ=0.988 at 20% replacement and 0.962 at 50% shows MMLU is massively redundant.\n      Reviewers use this against Claim 1: \"You've shown that removing 31% of items barely moves\n      rankings (ρ≈0.997 from plan_017). You've now shown that replacing up to 50% of items only\n      drops ρ to 0.962. So the 31% instability doesn't matter — the benchmark is robust to it.\n      Why should anyone care?\" The 4 subjects losing >50% items at 31% replacement is a minor\n      concern but reviewer notes these are small subjects (N<50 items) already statistically\n      unreliable. This experiment provides ammunition for the \"instability ≠ invalidity\"\n      framing but reviewers in worst tier turn it into \"instability AND invalidity are both\n      non-issues, so what's the contribution?\"\n    tables:\n      - \"Table: Ranking stability metrics by replacement rate — all high\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure: ρ degradation curve — nearly flat until 50%, then gentle decline\"\n    analysis: >\n      Graceful degradation is a double-edged sword. In best tiers, it supports the\n      \"instability ≠ invalidity\" framing as nuanced. In worst tier, it's used to argue the\n      entire paper investigates a non-problem. Reviewer: \"MMLU tolerates 50% item replacement\n      with ρ>0.96. Your 31% instability finding is interesting but inconsequential. The benchmark\n      works fine. Move on.\"\n\n  - name: item_focused_trees\n    plan_id: plan_009\n    description: \"Item-Focused Trees (Tutz & Berger 2016) — DIF with continuous release-date covariate\"\n    expected_results:\n      n_items_with_significant_splits: 3421\n      pct_items_with_splits: 27.4\n      concordance_with_mh_dif_c: 0.61\n      primary_split_variable: \"release_date\"\n      secondary_splits: \"model_size (19%), training_data_cutoff (12%)\"\n      tree_depth_median: 2\n      overfitting_cv_auc_drop: 0.14\n    result_rationale: >\n      In worst tier, Item-Focused Trees reveal that model release date is the primary split for\n      27.4% of items — consistent with temporal DIF but concordance with MH (0.61) is moderate,\n      not high. This opens an attack: \"If a more flexible method agrees with MH on only 61%\n      of flags, MH is either over- or under-detecting.\" Secondary splits on model_size (19%)\n      and training_data_cutoff (12%) suggest DIF is partially confounded with these covariates,\n      not purely temporal. The cv_auc drop of 0.14 indicates overfitting, which reviewers use\n      to question tree-based results. Most damagingly, reviewer notes: \"If release date is the\n      primary DIF driver and model size is secondary, you're detecting capability scaling, not\n      contamination or instability. These trees just rediscover that bigger, newer models do\n      better on certain items.\" The continuous-covariate approach, intended to avoid arbitrary\n      cohort splits, instead reveals that the underlying signal is smooth capability growth,\n      not discrete item-level dysfunction.\n    tables:\n      - \"Table: Split variable importance across items — release_date dominates\"\n      - \"Table: MH vs IFT flag concordance matrix\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure: Exemplar item trees showing release_date and model_size splits — reviewer\n        reads as 'scaling laws, not contamination'\"\n    analysis: >\n      Item-Focused Trees are a robustness check that in worst tier becomes a liability. The\n      moderate MH concordance and the model_size secondary splits give reviewers a clean\n      alternative narrative: \"DIF detects where models improved, which correlates with when\n      they were released and how big they are. This is capability scaling, observable from\n      accuracy trends alone, and requires no psychometric machinery.\" The IFT results\n      paradoxically make the simpler explanation (capability growth) more plausible than the\n      DIF interpretation (item-level functional instability).\n\n  - name: architecture_stratified_dif\n    plan_id: plan_010\n    description: \"DIF within decoder-only subpopulation to test IRT fit improvement when architecture is homogeneous\"\n    expected_results:\n      n_decoder_only: 5226\n      n_non_decoder: 1\n      pct_decoder_only: 99.98\n      c_pct_decoder_only: 31.3\n      c_pct_full: 31.3\n      jaccard_decoder_only_vs_full: 1.0\n      irt_rmsea_decoder_only: 0.089\n      irt_rmsea_full: 0.091\n      irt_misfit_pct_decoder_only: 97.8\n      fit_improvement: \"negligible\"\n    result_rationale: >\n      In worst tier, this experiment backfires. The finding that METABENCH MMLU is 99.98%\n      decoder-only (5226/5227) means the architecture stratification is trivial — removing\n      one model changes nothing (Jaccard=1.0). Reviewer: \"This isn't an experiment, it's a\n      data description. You showed your dataset has one non-decoder model. The fact that you\n      needed a whole experiment to discover this suggests insufficient dataset exploration\n      before running analyses.\" More importantly, IRT misfit barely improves (97.8% vs 98.0%)\n      in the decoder-only subset, confirming that architecture heterogeneity was never the\n      source of misfit — multidimensionality is. This should have been the paper's primary\n      concern from the start. Reviewer: \"Architecture homogeneity doesn't help because the\n      problem is 57 subject domains, not architecture. You've ruled out the wrong confounder.\"\n    tables:\n      - \"Table: Architecture composition of METABENCH — one row, one number\"\n    figures_description: []\n    analysis: >\n      Plan_010 was designed as a proactive defense against the IRT misfit concern. In worst\n      tier, it proves the defense is irrelevant: the IRT misfit isn't from architecture\n      heterogeneity (which doesn't exist) but from MMLU's inherent multidimensionality\n      (which can't be fixed by subsetting). The experiment answers the wrong question and\n      wastes precious page space on a tautological result. Reviewer correctly identifies\n      that the real issue — multidimensional IRT — is acknowledged but never attempted.\n\n  - name: semi_synthetic_contamination_validation\n    plan_id: plan_011\n    description: \"Inject known contamination into METABENCH response matrix, measure DIF detection power\"\n    expected_results:\n      n_clean_items_selected: 500\n      n_items_injected: 200\n      temporal_external_delta_tpr_p05: 0.508\n      temporal_external_delta_tpr_p10: 0.674\n      temporal_external_fpr: 0.314\n      dose_response_p03_tpr: 0.548\n      dose_response_p07_tpr: 0.990\n      dose_response_p10_tpr: 1.000\n      fpr_inflation_standard_mh: \"0.298→0.616 at p=1.0\"\n      matching_variable_contamination: true\n    result_rationale: >\n      In worst tier, the semi-synthetic results are numerically strong (ΔTPR=+0.508, clear dose-response)\n      but reviewers attack the injection mechanism itself. \"Flipping incorrect→correct with probability p\n      is a caricature of contamination. Real contamination manifests through training data exposure, which\n      can produce partial memorization, template matching, or reasoning shortcuts — none of which look\n      like a coin flip.\" The baseline FPR of 0.314 (31.4% of clean items flagged before injection) is\n      treated as disqualifying: \"Your method has a 31% false positive rate on items you selected as clean.\n      Any TPR increase is measured against an already-noisy baseline.\" The FPR inflation of standard MH\n      (0.298→0.616) when injection contaminates the matching variable is noted but reviewer argues:\n      \"This means your method is fragile to exactly the kind of correlated contamination that occurs in\n      practice.\" The contamination detection community reviewer: \"Semi-synthetic injection is the weakest\n      form of validation — we stopped using it years ago in favor of real contamination benchmarks.\n      This is validation theater.\"\n    tables:\n      - \"Table: ΔTPR dose-response curve — reviewer acknowledges monotonicity but questions mechanism\"\n      - \"Table: FPR inflation under standard vs external matching — reviewer focuses on baseline FPR\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure: Dose-response curve (injection rate vs TPR) — clean monotonic increase, but\n        reviewer annotates the 31% baseline FPR line\"\n    analysis: >\n      Plan_011 is the paper's strongest controlled experiment, but in worst tier it's\n      undermined by three attacks: (1) injection mechanism unrealistic, (2) baseline FPR=31%\n      meaning the method flags ~1/3 of clean items, (3) matching variable contamination\n      shows fragility. The 31% baseline FPR is the same 31% temporal C% from plan_001 —\n      reviewer connects these: \"Your 'semi-synthetic clean items' still show 31% DIF because\n      your method has a 31% base rate. Injection just adds more flags on top. The dose-response\n      proves injection works as a perturbation, not that DIF detects contamination.\" This is\n      the most sophisticated attack in the worst tier — it reframes the entire semi-synthetic\n      framework as measuring perturbation sensitivity, not contamination detection.\n```","stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"0cc9e7fb-63a4-4d71-be25-3b8e3d4642f2","total_cost_usd":0.4101225,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":19580,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"output_tokens":10686,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":19580,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":10686,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation_input_tokens":19580,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":19580},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":14547,"outputTokens":24,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.014667,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000},"claude-opus-4-6":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":10686,"cacheReadInputTokens":11841,"cacheCreationInputTokens":19580,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.3954555,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":64000}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","uuid":"3d98c6bc-476c-4f93-84b3-64f59cd4c69c"}
